{
  "version": 1,
  "id": "classical-bit-to-qubit",
  "title": "Classical Bit → Qubit",
  "description": "A synchronized masterclass tracing the transition from classical binary logic to quantum superposition and the Hadamard transformation.",
  "concepts": ["classical-bit", "qubit", "superposition", "hadamard-gate", "measurement"],
  "misconceptions": ["superposition-not-mixture", "measurement-deterministic", "hadamard-inverts-classical"],
  "defaults": {
    "duration": 1.5,
    "color": "#F8FAFC"
  },
  "actions": [
    {
      "type": "NARRATE",
      "text": "A classical bit has one definite value: zero or one. Today, we bridge the gap between classical computing and the quantum world."
    },
    {
      "type": "WRITE",
      "id": "heading-main",
      "text": "FROM CLASSICAL BIT TO QUANTUM QUBIT",
      "x": 230,
      "y": 70,
      "duration": 3.5,
      "style": {
        "fontSize": 34,
        "fontWeight": "bold",
        "color": "#38BDF8"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "UNDERLINE",
      "id": "ul-main",
      "x": 230,
      "y": 110,
      "width": 820,
      "duration": 1.0,
      "color": "#0284C7"
    },
    {
      "type": "PAUSE",
      "duration": 0.5
    },
    {
      "type": "CAPTION",
      "id": "cap-step1",
      "text": "Classical Computing: Deterministic | Quantum Computing: Superposition & Interference"
    },
    {
      "type": "NARRATE",
      "text": "In classical computing, a bit is deterministic. Like a physical light switch, it exists in only one definite state: either state zero, or state one."
    },
    {
      "type": "WRITE",
      "id": "col1-title",
      "text": "1. Classical Bit (Deterministic):",
      "x": 100,
      "y": 150,
      "duration": 2.2,
      "style": {
        "fontSize": 22,
        "fontWeight": "bold",
        "color": "#94A3B8"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "DRAW",
      "id": "box-classical",
      "shape": "rect",
      "x": 100,
      "y": 190,
      "w": 380,
      "h": 140,
      "filled": true,
      "duration": 1.5,
      "style": {
        "color": "#475569",
        "strokeWidth": 2.0
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "WRITE",
      "id": "text-classical-val",
      "text": "State is strictly 0 OR 1",
      "x": 130,
      "y": 230,
      "duration": 2.0,
      "style": {
        "fontSize": 22,
        "color": "#F8FAFC"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "WRITE",
      "id": "text-classical-example",
      "text": "Switch is either Open or Closed",
      "x": 130,
      "y": 275,
      "duration": 1.8,
      "style": {
        "fontSize": 18,
        "color": "#94A3B8"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "PAUSE",
      "duration": 0.5
    },
    {
      "type": "NARRATE",
      "text": "A quantum bit, or qubit, breaks this limitation. It can exist in a linear superposition of both zero and one simultaneously."
    },
    {
      "type": "WRITE",
      "id": "col2-title",
      "text": "2. Quantum Qubit (Superposition):",
      "x": 580,
      "y": 150,
      "duration": 2.2,
      "style": {
        "fontSize": 22,
        "fontWeight": "bold",
        "color": "#A78BFA"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "DRAW",
      "id": "box-qubit",
      "shape": "rect",
      "x": 580,
      "y": 190,
      "w": 600,
      "h": 140,
      "filled": true,
      "duration": 1.5,
      "style": {
        "color": "#7C3AED",
        "strokeWidth": 2.5
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "MATH",
      "id": "math-qubit-superpos",
      "expression": "|\\psi\\rangle = \\alpha|0\\rangle + \\beta|1\\rangle \\quad (|\\alpha|^2 + |\\beta|^2 = 1)",
      "x": 600,
      "y": 230,
      "duration": 2.8,
      "style": {
        "scale": 1.15,
        "fontSize": 22,
        "color": "#F5C842"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "WRITE",
      "id": "text-qubit-exp",
      "text": "Both basis states exist simultaneously with amplitudes α, β",
      "x": 600,
      "y": 285,
      "duration": 2.5,
      "style": {
        "fontSize": 17,
        "color": "#CBD5E1"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "HIGHLIGHT",
      "id": "hl-qubit-box",
      "x": 570,
      "y": 180,
      "width": 620,
      "height": 160,
      "duration": 0.8,
      "color": "rgba(124, 58, 237, 0.2)"
    },
    {
      "type": "PAUSE",
      "duration": 0.5
    },
    {
      "type": "NARRATE",
      "text": "To create superposition, we apply a Hadamard gate. The Hadamard transforms state zero into the plus state with equal fifty percent probabilities."
    },
    {
      "type": "WRITE",
      "id": "sec2-title",
      "text": "3. The Hadamard Gate (H):",
      "x": 100,
      "y": 380,
      "duration": 2.0,
      "style": {
        "fontSize": 24,
        "fontWeight": "bold",
        "color": "#34D399"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "MATH",
      "id": "math-hadamard-matrix",
      "expression": "H|0\\rangle = \\frac{|0\\rangle + |1\\rangle}{\\sqrt{2}} = |+\\rangle",
      "x": 100,
      "y": 440,
      "duration": 3.0,
      "style": {
        "scale": 1.25,
        "fontSize": 24,
        "color": "#F5C842"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "DRAW",
      "id": "circ-hadamard",
      "shape": "circle",
      "cx": 820,
      "cy": 450,
      "r": 50,
      "duration": 1.2,
      "style": {
        "color": "#38BDF8",
        "strokeWidth": 2.5
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "PAUSE",
      "duration": 0.5
    },
    {
      "type": "NARRATE",
      "text": "Upon measurement, the superposition immediately collapses into a single classical outcome with equal fifty percent probability."
    },
    {
      "type": "WRITE",
      "id": "sec3-measure",
      "text": "4. Measurement Collapse: P(0) = 50%,  P(1) = 50%",
      "x": 100,
      "y": 550,
      "duration": 3.0,
      "style": {
        "fontSize": 24,
        "fontWeight": "bold",
        "color": "#38BDF8"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "UNDERLINE",
      "id": "ul-measure",
      "x": 100,
      "y": 590,
      "width": 640,
      "duration": 1.0,
      "color": "#0284C7"
    },
    {
      "type": "PAUSE",
      "duration": 1.5
    }
  ]
}
